4.5 Revised design product
The improved pipeline’s three-way factorial is right in spirit and wrong in its first factor. The product we will actually interpret is (Figure 1)

\underbrace{\{\text{SILGGM+NPN},\ \text{PLN counts},\ \text{GmGM}\}}_{\text{three GGM families}}
\times
\underbrace{\{\text{affine},\ \text{scDesign2}\}}_{\text{two population lifts}}
\times
\underbrace{\{\mathcal{G}_{0},\ \text{optional NSGA-II}\}}_{\text{two panels}}.
\tag{4.7}
That is 12 routes. Bures–Wasserstein stays a perspective metric (Section 5.4), not a third lift. Independent type-level fits are the core sharing strategy; fused / copula-joint sharing stays in Section 4.6. SCTransform and huge.npn are not crossed with PLN. BLGGM is not in the product.
Scripts (when implemented): scripts/03_01_grn_silggm.R and a PLN script still to be added. No code is given here.

5.3 Affine lift and the precision-sparsity caveat

5.3.1 Independent Gaussian cells
Assume, for a fixed type j (subscript dropped),

\boldsymbol{x}_{c}
\sim
\mathcal{N}_{G}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{c},\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{c})
\quad\text{independent in }c.
\tag{5.3}
Affine closure of multivariate Gaussians (linear transformation theorem) gives

\widetilde{\boldsymbol{x}}
=
\sum_{c} w_{c}\boldsymbol{x}_{c}
\sim
\mathcal{N}_{G}\!\left(
\sum_{c}w_{c}\boldsymbol{\mu}_{c},\;
\sum_{c}w_{c}^{2}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{c}
\right).
\tag{5.4}
